sion was justified per the reviewer's instructions.\n\nHowever, there's a tension: the reviewer classified all 4 as \"Minor\" severity but still gave NEEDS REVISION. The review-findings.json codes them all as \"blocking\" severity. The output-v1.md puts them under \"Style (Should Fix)\" not \"Critical (Must Fix)\". This is consistent with the output template which separates critical from style issues but still allows style issues to be blocking.\n\nThe reviewer prompt says: \"One clear rule violation is enough to reject.\" So even minor but clear violations justify needs-revision.\n\nLooking at the severity classifications more carefully, the prompt specifies that STY items should be coded as blocking by default while SUG items are non-blocking. So the JSON classification is actually correct per the contract. The markdown uses a different severity system with Critical versus Minor distinctions, but these two systems coexist—the blocking/non-blocking distinction in the JSON maps to the STY/SUG categorization, while the markdown severity reflects the impact level within each category.\n\nInterestingly, this was the only reviewer across all three panels who requested revisions, which raises the question of whether the assessment was overly stringent. IA reviewer approved, the technical reviewer approved. But the style reviewer found genuine passive voice violations. The style guide clearly prohibits passive voice. This is within the reviewer's mandate.\n\nThe revision caused:\n1. Writer v2 to re-implement TASK-01 \n2. Validator v2 to re-validate\n3. Style reviewer v2 to re-review (solo, efficient)\n\nThis is expensive (extra revision cycle) but the findings were legitimate.\n\n**Efficiency of v2 re-review:**\n- 42 tool calls, 14 LLM turns — much leaner than the parallel group\n- Only view, edit, bash, create, skill, report_intent tools — focused\n- No errors\n- Verified all findings with a clean table\n- Correctly approved the revision\n\nThis is very efficient for a re-review.\n\n**Quality of v3 review (TASK-02):**\nThe reviewer ran through a comprehensive 17-check checklist and showed good scoping discipline by flagging pre-existing issues as out of scope. There's an interesting nuance with the en-dash finding—the reviewer's own prompt mentioned en dashes should render as `--`, but the actual style guide skill doesn't address this at all. The reviewer correctly deferred to the skill as the authoritative source rather than relying on its own checklist, which aligns with the instruction that skills are the authority.\n\nLooking at the artifact quality, status.json has all required fields and correctly references TASK-02, though the artifacts list only includes v3 files when v1 and v2 files also exist. The manifest covers all three iterations properly, and both review-findings-v2.json and review-findings-v3.json show approved verdicts with no findings.\n\nThe naming convention is slightly inconsistent—v1 uses \"review-findings.json\" without a version suffix while v2 and v3 use versioned names. The MCP SSE disconnections at the end appear to be session shutdown artifacts rather than actual errors. What's interesting is how the reviewer handled the en dash rule: even though its checklist mentions rendering en dashes as `--`, the style guide skill doesn't include this requirement, and the reviewer correctly deferred to the skills as the authority rather than flagging it in v3. That's solid judgment.\n\nFor STY-004, the reviewer flagged both the new \"Switch to\" instance and mentioned the pre-existing one for consistency, which technically goes beyond the diff-only scope, but the suggestion led to both being fixed anyway—an ideal outcome. Looking at the token usage, v1 and v3 were part of parallel batches so costs can't be isolated, but v2's solo span with 440K input and 6.7K output looks reasonable for a targeted re-review.\n\nThe v2 metrics show solid efficiency—42 tool calls with 16 compaction events at healthy utilization levels